<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover">
  <title>Returning to Aqua | Aquiplex</title>
  <link rel="icon" href="/logo/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/platform.css">
  <!--
    FALLBACK ONLY. In normal operation Android's verified App Link intercepts
    this URL before it ever renders. Reaching this page means that specific
    install's Digital Asset Links verification didn't succeed — most commonly
    a debug-signed build (different certificate than the published upload
    key, by design) rather than anything wrong with this deploy. The
    deepLink below is an explicit package="com.aquiplex.aqua" intent, which
    Android will still route to the app on tap regardless of verification
    state. See docs/OAUTH_NATIVE_HANDOFF.md.

    The one-time code is never interpolated into any visible text below —
    only, when present, into the deepLink href/redirect, which is not
    rendered as readable page content.
  -->
</head>
<body class="px-page">

  <main class="px-error-wrap">
    <h1>Returning to Aqua</h1>
    <p><%= message %></p>
    <div class="px-btn-row center">
      <% if (deepLink) { %>
        <a href="<%= deepLink %>" class="px-btn px-btn-primary">Open Aqua App</a>
      <% } else { %>
        <a href="/aqua" class="px-btn px-btn-primary">Open Aqua</a>
      <% } %>
    </div>
  </main>

<% if (deepLink) { %>
<script>
  // Best-effort auto-continue: most Chrome-on-Android builds honor an
  // intent: redirect without a user gesture. Browsers/WebViews that don't
  // (or block it) just leave the button above as the way forward — this
  // never throws if it's a no-op.
  window.location.href = <%- JSON.stringify(deepLink) %>;
</script>
<% } %>
<script src="/js/sw-cleanup.js" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
